---
import Home from '../../layouts/Home.astro';
import cn from 'clsx'
import { pageContainTwUtils } from '../../ui/spacing'

const TW_UTILS = {
  paragraph: cn("gap-6 px-6 py-8 sm:px-12 sm:py-12 lg:px-20")
}

const introParagraphUtils = {
  container: cn(
    TW_UTILS.paragraph,
    "flex flex-col",
    "text-xl",
    "max-w-full"
  )
}
---

<Home title="Why 0/0 does not equal 1" author="by Esteban Victorio" role="Tinkerer & Crafter of things">
  <div class="flex">
    <p class={introParagraphUtils.container}>
      <span class="w-1/2">
        Most likely, for some time now, Platzi will be the reference for the lessons learned while going through... well, Platzi.
      </span>

      <span class="w-1/2">
        And, honestly, this happens to be only because I don't really know where to start, haha.
        Platzi has worked for me in the past when going to the basics in tech, so, let's try something leaned more towards science itself.
      </span>
  
      <span class="w-1/2">
        Bluntly, I cannot say if this was due to me avoiding learning math until I felt... not necessarily ready for math, but rather in a mental state where I could just say: "well, I think I now have the right mindset to tackle what's hard about math."
      </span>

      <span class="w-1/2">
        But you didn't bother to pay a visit over here just to read the reasons as to why I avoided math, did you?
      </span>

      <span class="w-1/2">
        A random November day in 2026 (close to when I started writing this) I began taking the Math for Programming route in Platzi, which opens with the "Why 0/0 does not equal to 1".
      </span>

      <span class="w-1/2">
        Class is about 1 min and a few seconds long. And I said to myself: I don't think I'll be able to learn such an enigmatic puzzle in a very short time.
      </span>

      <span class="w-1/2">
        And I was right. BUT, that's not to say you are left with the clueless mind of your own to keep wondering and grounds you to a suspicion
        as to why you can doubt why it should not be equal to 1.
      </span>


      <span class="w-1/2">
        The issue goes like this: If 0/0 was equal to 1, then, we'd have to trust blindly the following as true as well:
      </span>

      <span class="w-1/2">
        If 0/0 is equal to 1, then we also know that 0/0 can be expressed as (0+0)/0.
      </span>

      <span class="w-1/2">
        Which, in turn, can also be expressed as 0/0 + 0/0, right? AND THEN, we'd have to also accept this:
      </span>

      <span class="w-1/2">
        0/0 + 0/0 is the same 1 + 1 by the prior statement. Which would ALSO be 1 + 1 = 2.
      </span>

      <span class="w-1/2">
        THEREFORE, 0/0 + 0/0 is the same 1 + 1 which is 2, AND THAT would be 2 = 0/0, since 0/0 was also (0+0)/0, which in turn would be 1 = 2.
      </span>

      <span class="w-1/2">
        And that's bullocks. It doesn't make sense.
      </span>


      <span>
        So, hopefully, something in the horizon can help us explain or demonstrate such thing, but now we have a clue.
      </span>
    </p>
  </div>
</Home>



